[test-suite name=code
  [doc [#
# CX code Conformance Suite
Version: 0.3 — 2026-05-24.

This file is the conformance home for CX code — the unified
pattern/query/transform language, normative per
[`spec/core/code.md`](../spec/core/code.md) and
[`spec/core/grammar.ebnf`](../spec/core/grammar.ebnf).

Iteration over CXPath uses `[?for]`; the closed `[?find]` directive
has no place in the surface. Fixture categories cover CXPath,
multi-arm `[?match]`, and `[?modify]` (categories below).

---

## Status

**Release gate #4** requires every directive, every parameter,
every error code in `spec/core/code.md` to be covered by at least
one fixture. Current coverage (this file) spans the full directive
surface.

---

## Format

Each fixture is a block of the form:


For fixtures expecting an error rather than output:


For fixtures with stdin / file inputs (services, concurrency), see
the format extensions in §Categories below.

### Fixture-only test helpers

State-machine and time-dependent fixtures (circuit-breaker
transitions, rate-limit replenishment, bulkhead concurrency,
retry-attempt counting) need deterministic inputs. The following
helpers are recognised **only by the conformance runner** and are
**not** part of the CX code language spec (they do not appear in
`spec/core/code.md` §4.1, do not appear in `spec/core/grammar.ebnf` [127e],
and MUST NOT be implementable as user code). A conformant
implementation runs the fixture against an interpreter mode that
maps these names to the runner's controllable primitives; outside
that mode, parsing `[?test-…]` is a `cx-err:CXER0100` error.

| Helper | Slots | Semantics |
|---|---|---|
| `[?test-seq values=(v1, v2, …)]` | `values` required | Returns `v1` on first call, `v2` on second, … cycles after the last value (so steady-state tests can drive past sequence end). Counter is per-helper-instance, scoped to the surrounding directive. |
| `[?test-err-then-ok err-count=N ok-value=V]` | `err-count`, `ok-value` required | Returns `[err code='test-failure' message='test failure']` for the first N calls, then `V` on every subsequent call. Equivalent shorthand for the common retry/circuit-breaker pattern. |
| `[?test-always-err]` | none | Returns `[err code='test-failure' message='test failure']` on every call. |
| `[?test-clock advance=DURATION]` | `advance` optional | Advances the mock clock by DURATION (e.g. `100ms`, `5s`). Without `advance`, returns the current mock-clock instant as an opaque value comparable with `=`. Mock clock starts at instant `0` at fixture start. |
| `[?test-concurrent tasks=(t1, t2, …)]` | `tasks` required | Schedules the given task bodies to run concurrently. Returns the sequence of their results in completion order. Used by bulkhead / async fixtures. |
| `[?test-counter name=STR op=(read \| inc)]` | `name` required, `op` default `read` | Named integer counter; `inc` increments and returns the new value, `read` returns the current value (0 if never incremented). |
| `[?test-cb-open]` | none | Returns the canonical breaker-open err: `[err code='cx-err:CXER0150' message='circuit breaker open' [until [instant 0]]]`. Lets composition-matrix cells drive a pre-tripped-circuit-breaker scenario without inline priming. |
| `[?test-rate-limited]` | none | Returns the canonical rate-limit err: `[err code='cx-err:CXER0151' message='rate limit exceeded' retry-after=100ms]`. |
| `[?test-bulkhead-full]` | none | Returns the canonical bulkhead-saturated err: `[err code='cx-err:CXER0152' message='bulkhead saturated' max=1]`. |
| `[?test-service-client [service S] [client-call C]]` | both required | Spawns service `S` on an OS-assigned free port, makes the host:port available to `C` via the bound `$test-target` URL, evaluates `C`, then `[?stop]`s the service. Returns C's result. Used by service+client round-trip fixtures so each fixture is a single CX program. |
| `[?test-tls-config]` | none | Returns a TLS-CONFIG value pointing at a self-signed cert + key the runner pre-generates per fixture-run. Used by TLS round-trip fixtures. |

The mock clock is deterministic: `[?sleep D mock]` and resilience-
directive backoff/timeout deadlines consume mock-clock time, not
wall-clock time. Fixture authors `[?test-clock advance=D]` to step
time past deadlines.

**All conformance fixtures use explicit `[?sleep DUR mock]`**.
Bare `[?sleep DUR]` is wall-clock by default in CX programs (see
[`spec/core/code.md §10.5.3`](../spec/core/code.md)); the conformance corpus
opts into mock-clock per fixture by writing `mock` explicitly.
The fixture author's intent — "this fixture depends on mock-clock
semantics for determinism" — is then visible in each test rather
than implicit in a runner mode. The deferred gate-39 fixtures
(below) keep bare `[?sleep DUR]` precisely because they verify
wall-clock behaviour; they require harness extensions.

---

## Categories

- **core** — patterns, paths, comprehensions, conditionals, let,
  fn, def, try, pipe.
- **resilience** — retry, timeout, circuit-breaker, fallback,
  rate-limit, bulkhead.
- **services** — service + client, request/response round-trips,
  HTTP error responses.
- **concurrency** — workers, channels, select, send/receive.
- **async** — async, await, await-all/any/race, cancel.
- **visualization** — diagram round-trip per §11.1.
- **cxpath** — Path value kind: `//name`, predicates, axes, binding paths.
- **match-multi** — multi-arm `[?match]`: `[case]`, `[when]`, `[else]`, scalar literals, wildcard `_`, guards.
- **modify** — `[?modify]` action vocabulary: `[set]`, `[delete]`, `[using]`, `[rename]`, `[set-attr]`, `[delete-attr]`, `[append]`, `[prepend]`, `[insert-before]`, `[insert-after]`, `[replace]`.

---

